faq:
  - q: "What are LinkedIn keywords?"
    a: "LinkedIn keywords are the specific role titles, skills, technologies, certifications, and domain terms that recruiters type into LinkedIn's search bar when sourcing candidates. The LinkedIn algorithm weights your headline, current title, and skills endorsements most heavily. If the exact term is not in your profile, you will not appear in searches for that term."
  - q: "Where should keywords appear on your LinkedIn profile?"
    a: "Five locations, in order of algorithm weight: (1) Headline — most important, 220-character limit. (2) Current job title field. (3) Skills section — get endorsements for the highest-priority ones. (4) About section — include 5–8 terms in context. (5) Experience descriptions — mention relevant tools and skills explicitly, not just implicitly."
  - q: "How do I find the right LinkedIn keywords?"
    a: "Three methods: (1) Search LinkedIn for your target role and look at the profiles that appear first — what terms are in their headlines and skills? (2) Extract repeated terms from 10–15 target job descriptions. (3) Look at LinkedIn's 'People also viewed' profiles for your role at your target level. The terms appearing consistently are your priority keywords."
  - q: "How many skills should I list on LinkedIn?"
    a: "40–50 skills — LinkedIn allows up to 50. Fill it to capacity with relevant terms. Prioritize skills that appear in your target job descriptions and get colleagues to endorse the top 5–10 most important ones. Endorsed skills carry more weight in search ranking."
  - q: "Does keyword stuffing work on LinkedIn?"
    a: "Keyword density matters, but stuffing backfires. A headline that is just a string of skills ('Python | AWS | Kubernetes | DevOps | SRE | Cloud') looks spammy and reduces click-through rates even if it appears in search. The best profiles balance keyword density with readable positioning."
